b/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548020 #edb/ref 1069 "q-79428" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548020 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548020 #edb/ref 1071 "The point $P$ with coordinates $(-6,9)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x).$ Which of the following points must lie on $y=f(\\frac{1}{3}x)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548020 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7ec57449-f520-50b3-b823-1f98b4f880f3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548020 #edb/ref 1074 "The function $y=f(\\frac{1}{3}x)$ represents a stretch of the function $y=f(x)$ by a factor of $3$ parallel to the $x$-axis. This has the effect of multiplying the $x$-coordinates of all points on $y=f(x)$ by $3.$\n\nTherefore, the point $(-6,9)$ on $f(x)$ is mapped to the point $(3\\cdot (-6),9)=(-18,9),$ and this point must lie on the graph of $y=f(\\frac{1}{3}x)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548062 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186547907 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548062 #edb/ref 1069 "q-79427" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548062 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548062 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/75/75f479ff700f106f5cae6bafa94df87381d5fc0fca1758aad55f6bc5be1d7c38.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following is the graph of $y=f(\\frac{1}{4}x)?$\n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548062 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d363b88c-fb75-5b26-bd10-c56875f40c1e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548062 #edb/ref 1074 "To graph the function $y=f(\\frac{1}{4}x),$ we take the graph of $y=f(x)$ and stretch it by factor of $4$ parallel to the $x$-axis. This has the effect of multiplying the $x$-coordinates of all points on $y=f(x)$ by $4.$\n\nThe resulting graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/0f/0f3cf395fe5f442212e057f73ef48ab458370e914424e186611c70f1c9f29953.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548094 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186548129 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548094 #edb/ref 1062 